CAT = "sgsnbSurgical"; SEG = "SUR"
Q = [
('Which nursing action belongs to the preoperative checks on the ward?',
 'Confirm signed consent and marked site', ['Give a light snack shortly before theatre', 'Leave dentures in place for theatre', 'Check the identity band after theatre'],
 'The nurse verifies identity, a signed consent, the marked surgical site, fasting status, allergies and removal of jewellery and dentures per policy.'),
('Why is a patient kept fasted before an operation under general anaesthesia?',
 'To reduce the risk of aspiration', ['To lower blood pressure', 'To speed wound healing', 'To reduce the need for analgesia'],
 'Food or fluid in the stomach can be regurgitated and inhaled while protective airway reflexes are depressed.'),
('Which postoperative finding needs urgent action in the first hours?',
 'Rising pulse with falling blood pressure', ['Mild sleepiness after the anaesthetic', 'A dry mouth and thirst', 'Mild nausea that settles'],
 'Tachycardia with hypotension suggests haemorrhage or shock, which needs early escalation.'),
('How does deep breathing and coughing help after surgery?',
 'It prevents lung collapse and infection', ['It reduces the size of the wound', 'It slows the heart rate down', 'It lowers the potassium level'],
 'Deep breathing, coughing and early mobilisation reopen alveoli and clear secretions, lowering the risk of pneumonia.'),
('A patient should splint the abdominal wound when coughing. How?',
 'Hold a pillow firmly over the incision', ['Lie flat and hold his breath', 'Avoid coughing completely after surgery', 'Press only with the fingertips'],
 'Supporting the wound with a pillow reduces pain and strain on the sutures while coughing.'),
('Which sign of wound infection should a nurse report?',
 'Spreading redness, heat and pus', ['Slight bruising along the edges', 'A thin pink line at the incision', 'Itching only during the second week'],
 'Spreading erythema, warmth, swelling, pain and pus, often with fever, suggest infection.'),
('What is the aim of a moist wound healing dressing?',
 'To keep the wound moist and protected', ['To dry the wound as quickly as possible', 'To prevent any wound drainage', 'To avoid ever changing the dressing'],
 "A moist environment supports cell migration and autolytic debridement; the dressing is chosen for the wound's exudate and tissue type."),
('Which wound would normally be managed by healing by secondary intention?',
 'A deep open wound filling from the base', ['A clean incision closed with sutures', 'A skin graft site sutured at once', 'A small, neat paper cut to a finger'],
 'Secondary intention means the wound is left open and heals by granulation and contraction, as in many pressure injuries or infected wounds.'),
('A surgical drain with suction contains 150 mL of bright red blood in one hour. What should the nurse do?',
 'Report it to the surgeon at once', ['Empty it and record it at the end of shift', 'Clamp the drain to stop the loss', 'Remove the drain to check the wound'],
 'A sudden large bloody output suggests active bleeding and requires immediate medical review.'),
('Which nutrient is most important for wound healing?',
 'Protein', ['Dietary fibre', 'Saturated fat', 'Sodium'],
 'Protein, vitamin C and zinc are needed for tissue repair, so adequate nutrition is part of wound care.'),
('Which condition delays wound healing?',
 'Poorly controlled diabetes', ['Good hydration', 'Adequate protein intake', 'Moist wound dressings'],
 'High glucose impairs white cell function and circulation, delaying healing and raising infection risk.'),
('Which blood glucose is the usual threshold that defines hypoglycaemia?',
 'Below 4 mmol/L', ['Below 7 mmol/L', 'Below 10 mmol/L', 'Below 2 mmol/L'],
 'Hypoglycaemia is usually defined as a blood glucose below 4 mmol/L, with treatment starting at that level.'),
('A conscious diabetic patient with a glucose of 3.2 mmol/L is sweating and shaky. What is the first treatment?',
 'Give 15 to 20 g of quick sugar', ['Give a large meal straight away', 'Give his next dose of insulin early', 'Wait and recheck in one hour'],
 'A quick carbohydrate source, such as glucose tablets or juice, is given, glucose is rechecked in about 15 minutes and a longer-acting snack follows.'),
('Which sign is typical of diabetic ketoacidosis?',
 'Fruity breath, deep rapid breathing', ['Cold, damp skin with a tremor', 'Slow, shallow, quiet breathing', 'Pale skin with a very low glucose'],
 'Ketones cause a fruity odour and the acidosis causes deep, rapid (Kussmaul) breathing, with high glucose and dehydration.'),
('Which test shows average blood glucose control over the past two to three months?',
 'HbA1c', ['Fasting lipid profile', 'Random urine ketones', 'Serum amylase'],
 'Glycated haemoglobin reflects average glucose over the red cell lifespan of about three months.'),
('Where should insulin pens and vials in current use be stored?',
 'Room temperature, away from sunlight', ['In the freezer compartment', 'In the refrigerator door at all times', 'Beside a warm kitchen appliance'],
 'Unopened insulin is refrigerated; the in-use vial or pen is kept at room temperature away from heat and light for the time stated by the manufacturer.'),
('Which advice protects the feet of a patient with diabetes?',
 'Check feet daily; wear closed shoes', ['Walk barefoot to improve circulation', 'Remove corns with over-the-counter plasters', 'Soak the feet in hot water daily'],
 'Neuropathy and poor circulation hide injuries, so daily inspection, well-fitting shoes and prompt treatment prevent ulcers.'),
('Which rotation approach is correct for insulin injection sites?',
 'Rotate sites within the same body area', ['Always use the same spot for consistency', 'Inject only into muscle', 'Inject only after massaging the site'],
 'Rotating sites prevents lipohypertrophy, which causes erratic absorption.'),
('After stoma surgery, which stoma colour is expected in the early days?',
 'Pink to red and moist', ['Dark purple or black', 'Pale and dry', 'Grey and shrunken'],
 'A healthy stoma is pink or red and moist; a dusky purple or black stoma suggests poor blood supply and is reported urgently.'),
('Which solution is generally used to clean an uncomplicated wound?',
 'Normal saline', ['Undiluted hydrogen peroxide', 'Strong povidone iodine every time', 'Alcohol-based rub'],
 'Sterile normal saline cleans a wound without damaging healthy granulation tissue; harsh antiseptics can delay healing.'),
]
